ind** (both East) if you can pung it. But only one F1 unshown (you hold it), and no other F1 discarded or melded? \n  - Previous player melded F2,F2,F2\n  - Across played F1 once\n  - Next played F1 twice\n  - You played F3, F3, F4, F4 \u2014 but not F1\n  - You have F1:1 in hand\n  - Unshown F1:1 \u2192 so only one exists unshown \u2192 cannot form pung \u2192 cannot use for wind pattern.\n\nSo **cannot** get Prevalent Wind or Seat Wind points \u2014 only one F1 left, and you can't get three.\n\nSimilarly, dragons: J1, J2 all gone \u2014 J3 has two unshown \u2014 but you don't have any.\n\nSo dragon patterns impossible.\n\nNow, look at sequences:\n- You have T3, T4, T6 \u2014 close to Chow [T3,T4,T5] or [T4,T5,T6] \u2014 but T5 is rare (only one unshown), and next player has already made [T3,T4,T5] via Chi \u2014 so T5 likely used.\n- B6, B7, B8 \u2014 beautiful sequence! Just need B5 or B9 to complete [B5,B6,B7] or [B7,B8,B9]\n  - But B5: unshown 0 \u2014 all gone! Used by across player\u2019s [B5,B5,B5]\n  - B9: unshown 4 \u2014 plenty!\n  - So [B7,B8,B9] possible if you get B9\n\nAlso, B1, B3 \u2014 missing B2 (unshown 0 \u2014 all gone) \u2192 cannot form [B1,B2,B3]\n\nSo best path: aim for **Full Flush** or **Pure Straight** in Bamboos?\n\nBut you have many non-Bamboo tiles.\n\nAlternatively, **Mixed Shifted Chows**?\n\nBut better: consider **Half Flush** (Bamboos + Honors)?\n\nYou have:\n- B1, B3, B6, B7, B8\n- F1\n- And T3,T4,T6,W1,W2 \u2014 these are liabilities.\n\nBetter to reduce non-Bamboo.\n\nBut B6,B7,B8 is strong \u2014 potential chow.\n\nAlso, T3,T4,T6 \u2014 T3,T4,T5 gone, but T4,T5,T6? T5 gone.\n\nT3,T4,T6 \u2192 can become [T3,T4,T5] if T5 appears? No.\n\nOr wait \u2014 you could form [T4,T5,T6] if you get T5 \u2014 but only one T5 unshown, and it's still in wall? Possibly.\n\nBut risky.\n\nBetter to focus on **Bamboo-based Half Flush**.\n\nDiscard non-Bamboo tiles.\n\nBut F1 is honor \u2014 useful for Outside Hand or Melded Hand, but not strongly.\n\nNow, safest discard: avoid helping opponents.\n\nDangerous tiles:\n- T3: next player just used T3 in [T1,T1,T1] and earlier plays \u2014 maybe building something? But they have [T9,T9,T9],[T3,T4,T5],[T1,T1,T1] \u2014 possibly going for All Terminals or similar. T3 might be safe.\n- T4: they used in Chow \u2014 probably safe.\n- T6: not seen in their melds \u2014 could be dangerous? But they have many terminals \u2014 T6 is simple.\n- W1/W2: you have only one each unshown \u2014 but you hold them \u2014 others unlikely to need.\n- B1/B3: across player has [B1,B2,B3] \u2014 so they have B1 and B3 \u2014 so discarding B1 or B3 is dangerous! They might want another pung.\n- Similarly, previous player has [B4,B4,B4],[B2,B2,B2] \u2014 so B1 and B3 might not help them directly, but across does.\n\nSo **B1 and B3 are dangerous** \u2014 do not discard.\n\nB6: unshown 3 \u2014 safe\nB7: unshown 3 \u2014 safe\nB8: unshown 4 \u2014 very safe\n\nF1: only one unshown \u2014 you hold it \u2014 others may not have it, but prev and next have discarded F2,F3,F4 \u2014 so F1 might be safe? But East Wind \u2014 sometimes kept.\n\nStill, only one exists \u2014 hard to use.\n\nBut **F1 can be used in honors-only melds**, but no one seems to be building that.\n\nBest discard: **B8**\n\nWhy?\n- Part of potential [B7,B8,B9] \u2014 but B9 is abundant (4 unshown), so you can draw B9 later.\n- B8 has 4 copies unshown \u2014 all still in wall? Wall has limited tiles, but many B8 unseen \u2014 likely safe.\n- Keeping B6,B7,B9 gives chance for [B6,B7,B8] or [B7,B8,B9] if draw B9.\n- But you have B8 now \u2014 if you discard it, you lose initiative.\n\nWait \u2014 you want to keep B6,B7,B8 together \u2014 strong sequence.\n\nSo why discard B8? Better to keep it.\n\nThen discard a terminal or honor.\n\nBut B1 and B3 are dangerous.\n\nT3, T4, T6: T-suit \u2014 next player has many T, but they seem to be in terminal phase \u2014 T3,T4,T6 are simples \u2014 possibly safe.\n\nW1, W2: Characters \