ardless, the **unshown tiles** list is authoritative: W1:1 unshown.\n\nSo one W1 remains undiscarded and not in melds.\n\nCurrently, you have W1:1 in hand \u2192 so you hold it.\n\nThen the other three W1s must be already discarded or in other melds.\n\nBut no other player has W1 in their melds.\n\nSo three W1s have been discarded.\n\nTherefore, discarding your last W1 is safe \u2014 no one can use it.\n\nSimilarly, W2: unshown 1, you hold it, others have discarded W2? You played W2 once, next played W2 once, previous played W2 twice \u2014 total 4? But only 4 exist.\n\nYou: played W2 once\nNext: played W2 once\nPrevious: played W2 twice? History: \u201cPlay T2\u201d multiple times \u2014 T2, not W2.\n\nW2: you played W2? In history: \u201cPlay W2\u201d \u2014 no.\n\nYou played: W9, W8, W6, etc., but not W2.\n\nMeld used W2 (from discard), so you didn't provide W2.\n\nYou have W2:1 in hand now \u2014 so you drew it later.\n\nWho discarded W2? Must be someone.\n\nBut anyway, unshown W2:1 \u2192 you hold it \u2192 safe to discard.\n\nBut W2 is part of a potential second Chow in W? You already have [W1,W2,W3] melded, so another W1,W2,W3 not useful.\n\nBut you have W1 and W2 in hand \u2014 could form another Chow if you get W3 \u2014 but W3 unshown:0 \u2014 all gone.\n\nSo cannot.\n\nSo W1 and W2 in hand are useless.\n\nBetter to discard one.\n\nBut which?\n\nF1: same issue \u2014 only one, cannot form pung.\n\nSo all single honors and terminals are candidates.\n\nBut B6,B7,B8 is promising.\n\nKeep B6,B7,B8 together.\n\nT3,T4,T6: T3 and T4 could form [T3,T4,T5] if T5 appears \u2014 but T5 unshown:1 \u2014 possible.\n\nBut less likely than getting B9 for [B7,B8,B9].\n\nB9 unshown:4 \u2014 high chance.\n\nSo prioritize B-chows.\n\nDiscard a non-B tile.\n\nBest discard: **F1**\n\nWhy?\n- Cannot form pung (only one exists)\n- Not needed for any pair\n- Honor tile, but not helping much\n- Safer than discarding B1/B3 (dangerous)\n- Safer than T3/T4 (might help next player)\n\nT3: next player has [T3,T4,T5] \u2014 already completed \u2014 so T3 is safe to discard.\n\nSimilarly T4.\n\nBut F1 is even safer.\n\nFinal decision: **Play F1**\n\nReason: F1 is an isolated wind tile with no possibility of forming a pung (only one copy unshown, held by me), and it does not contribute to any strong pattern. Discarding it avoids giving away potentially useful suited tiles like B1 or B3, which could help opponents who have already formed melds in those suits (e.g., across player has [B1,B2,B3]). It preserves flexibility in the stronger-suited tiles.\n\nAnswer: Play F1"}
